i tried to sit back as far as i could so  there'd be a long awkward silence  thank you to dr arn the platform party  family and friends  and to the class of 2015 congratulations  we made it  before i begin my speech i find it  appropriate to brag on behalf of this  class to all of you here  the class of 2015  is the smartest class ever admitted to  hillsdale college  and as of last night it is also the most  philanthropic  last summer the other class officers and  i set a goal for 50 percent class  participation in our give back campaign  and according to my count we've reached  our goal  once we finalize these pledges several  generous alumni will help us establish a  scholarship for next year's incoming  freshmen as a token of our love for this  place  and uh and one more note  two years ago  at r.a training dr aaron made a deal  with matt o'sullivan that if he kept his  four point dr arn would buy him any book  up to a certain amount  and as matt is too humble to say this  for himself i feel obligated to remind  the good doctor of this  so  matthew what was the amount  100  i'm losing money like crazy so  so send me an email and remind me and  i'll send you the book for the hundred  dollars the um  i made a bet with jack babbitt he's  gonna watch this tape  and he gave a big gift for the chapel  and with the architect sitting there and  with rich payway sitting there he said  you'll never build this chapel  for this amount it's going to cost four  times as much  and i said in a rash moment  i bet you  five thousand dollars to your favorite  charity against five thousand dollars to  mind that we can build it for that and i  looked at rich  and i looked at  the architect and they both nodded their  heads  and i'm getting ready to write a check  to jack babbitt for five thousand  dollars  now it's five thousand one 100.  thank you dr arn  when i began to write this speech last  night  i'm really glad you all thought that was  a joke  i considered several different  approaches after all  what is one supposed to talk about in a  commencement speech  should i reflect on the last four years  here  reminiscing about the highlights of my  experience  an attempt to generalize them so that  everyone might identify  not to say that there's anything wrong  or at all inaccurate about remembering  those nights when the cell phones go  silent the laptops are shut tight and  the conversation amongst friends soars  far and wide  after all it is true that most of us if  not all of us have had those experiences  and that is a good thing  but this is a commencement address  for a while now i have found it odd that  we call the graduation of seniors in the  spring commencement dr ward pointed this  out  commencement refers to a beginning  to make a start or enter in upon  something  yet we are concluding our education here  and our time has come to an end  of course many of you probably want to  respond with but our education never  ends to which i'd respond you're right  now shut up i'm giving the speech  so why do we name the ceremony marking  our departure from the school this place  in these people in terms of a beginning  well i have a theory  commencement is an attempt to give this  moment a particular significance  in a conscious manner we want this  moment of transition to be one of hope  and of promise a setting out from home  and confidence and joy towards some  glorious fate  this day is the first day of the rest of  our lives and four years here have  prepared us to meet it with bright eyes  and full hearts  if only that were true  in order to make us even more  self-conscious about what is happening  right here right now i'll tell you how  i'm actually  am terrified  the last time that i was this terrified  was one thousand three hundred fifty  days ago  august twenty eight two thousand eleven  in our freshman convocation  i'll be honest with you  like many here today i had not wanted to  come to hillsdale in fact it was my last  choice  some of you including my family knew  that  but until now i've never told anyone the  real reason why  it wasn't because of pride or  stubbornness or some other pretended  thing  i didn't come to this school because i  was terrified  terrified to fail  terrified to succeed  terrified of the unknown  this college is hard what we do here is  difficult from beginning to end without  exception  and the worst part is that it is  completely up to us to do it  sure we have the support of so many  wonderful people  but the fundamental responsibility lies  with us  the words of albert camus from dr  jackson's great books class come to mind  i didn't know that freedom is not a  reward or a decoration that is  celebrated with champagne  nor yet a gift a box of dainties to make  you look your chops  oh no it's a chore on the contrary and a  long distance race  quite solitary and very exhausting  at the end of all at the end of all  excuse me  freedom is a court sentence  that's why freedom is too heavy to bear  especially when you're down with a fever  or are distressed  or love nobody  1 350 days ago we stood together at east  lawn  and at the prompting of dr arn we faced  our parents and loved ones hugged them  and walked up to that hill to find those  mentor groups we now laughingly  remember and we began our journey  today 1 350 days later we find ourselves  together again  and today  i'm still terrified  terrified to fail terrified to succeed  terrified of the unknown  and terrified because i'm about to leave  the best friends for which a man could  ever hope  last year josh andrew asked on this  occasion was it worth it  this year i asked the question is that  whatever we are leaving this for worth  it  and my answer is simple  yes and no  no because a few of us just a few of us  will ever have another chance to study  these great things to spend our days in  leisure and contemplation and enjoy the  immediate presence of friends engaged in  the same activity of soul  not to mention that if we were to  continue all the stupid things we've  done here most of us would live in boxes  without a prospect of a real job  and i say yes  that whatever is out there is worth  leaving this for  because we were never meant to stay here  the hard truth of this college is that  we live and learn here for a short time  during which we are formed and molded to  live in the world  we are called from here to love and to  teach others  to show them the beauty that we have  been shown to suffer and struggle and do  the difficult activity of living  often it will be thankless  often it will be suffering  always though  it should be joyous  we are called and we must respond  the funny thing about what we are doing  here today is that no matter how hard we  try no matter how flowing our robes are  or how many pictures we take we cannot  give this day meaning  but that isn't to say that today isn't  significant because in a very real way  this is the most significant day of our  lives  for the first time in four years we are  gathered together in one place  we've all grown and flourished in a  variety of astounding ways  so this moment is not significant  because of the robes or the pictures or  because we want it to be  the significance and overwhelming  meaning of this moment right here right  now  is not the result of our conscious  choice to make it so  but comes from all our experiences until  now  and now we stand at the end of this  journey  staring into what may seem to be an  abyss but this abyss should not terrify  us but give us hope  because in its darkness is not futility  and nothingness but endless possibility  for greatness  the unknown is just that  unknown  we can only choose how we greet it with  fear and trembling  or with wonder  so i return to the words of camus  at the end of all freedom is a court  sentence  that's why freedom is too heavy to bear  especially when you're down with a fever  or are distressed  or love nobody  and i think that this is right because  after four years here we have found  freedom to be a chore it has been a long  distance race at times quite solitary  and very exhausting and far too heavy to  bear  this is the fate to which we have  dedicated ourselves and our commitment  to this college  and to each other  but here at the end of it all this  realization is the moment to which our  education has led us  to recognize the difficulty of what we  have chosen to to do and not merely to  face it but to thrive in the face of it  we have not simply overcome suffering  we have been formed by it for a future  far more glorious than any we can  conceive  we need only look back at those men and  women who helped raise us  our parents  for a model of this  consider what it means to bear a child  bringing into this world new life a  helpless and vulnerable person for whom  you are wholly responsible this burden  is terrifying  they are here today not because of fear  for responsibility but out of love for  the life that they have helped form and  in the process been formed by  they recognize the beauty of this moment  not because it isn't  a moment without strife and despair  but they recognize the beauty of this  moment  because they've been they've persevered  through those things  and they have learned to love  that which is true and good  from this moment forward we will share  in this beauty and not because it isn't  suffering or pain or struggle but  because through those things our hearts  and souls have been formed to a greater  love of that which is good and beautiful  after all  one sees clearly only with the heart  anything essential is invisible to the  eyes  so today i thank god and i thank all of  you  because we know that this freedom we  undertake is not too heavy  we love just as we are loved  and we have the people around us today  our family  our friends  and each other  to thank for that  as we gather here together again  i have faith this love will  abide through it  we will know beauty  and through it  we have a hope that leads us beyond the  confines of this world  you

